NARRATIVE = {
    "question": """<p>On trajectories that pass through free motion, contact onset, sustained pressing and sliding, and release, at several speeds and payloads, do the learned controllers beat fixed gains and the conventional best practice of phase-specific gains with switching and interpolation? The reference is held fixed; only the controller changes.</p>""",
    "setup": """<p>Cases are the sustained-contact and transition references of protocol v1 (table and wall) at speed scales 1, 1.5 and 2 and payloads of 0 and 1 kg (8 test cases per cell). The phase-scheduled baseline uses the Milestone 1 per-regime gains (transition gains during approach, release and free phases; contact gains during pressing and sliding), switched at the reference's phase boundaries (oracle phase labels) with linear interpolation over 0, 50 or 200 ms. Learned controllers are the Milestone 2 (nominal) and Milestone 3 (DR) variants, evaluated unchanged.</p>""",
    "results_intro": """<p>Twelve cells (two regimes, three speeds, two payloads) with eight test cases each; every controller sees the identical reference in every cell. Impulse, settling and release metrics refer to the first contact onset of the episode; the sustained-contact references additionally contain a 12 cm slide.</p>""",
    "findings": """<ol>
<li><strong>Phase-scheduled gains with oracle switching reduce impact forces at high approach speed, at a price.</strong> Without payload the 200 ms blend cuts the transition peak force from 173 to 79 N at 1.5 times the nominal speed and from 241 to 82 N at twice the speed (the fixed transition-tuned gains reach 38 to 40 N, but by not pressing at all, mean force 1 to 2 N). The hard switch produces peak torque rates of 40 to 77 thousand Nm/s (60 to 100 times those of the fixed gains) and force variation of 10 to 25 N during pressing; the 50 ms blend brings the torque rate to 1.4 to 6 thousand Nm/s, the 200 ms blend to 0.5 to 2.4 thousand, comparable to fixed gains, but the long blend delays tracking recovery after release to 0.5 to 0.6 s against 0.03 to 0.05 s for fixed gains.</li>
<li><strong>Switching into the compliant gains fails with a 1 kg payload.</strong> The transition-tuned gains (11 and 5 Nm/rad on the gravity-loaded joints) let the arm sag under the payload: position RMSE 45 to 49 mrad, peak force 187 to 197 N and a composite of 8 to 9 for every scheduled variant, against 2.6 to 5.9 for the shared gains. A gain schedule tuned on the nominal plant is the least robust controller in the grid.</li>
<li><strong>The learned controllers (protocol v1) are close to the shared gains on this compositional test.</strong> Learned gains reduce the onset impulse by 20 to 30% in transitions (4.8 against 6.5 N s at nominal speed) with equal peak force, and have the lowest force variation during pressing (0.5 to 1.2 N), but lose contact more often while sliding (7 to 24 losses per case against 0.25 to 13 for the shared gains) and track the slide less accurately (8.7 against 7.0 mm). Neural torque lowers the peak force and impulse (76 N and 4.2 N s at nominal speed) by pressing less, with peak torque rates of 20 to 30 thousand Nm/s. PD + residual is the shared PD. The DR variants behave like their nominal twins.</li>
<li><strong>Payload and speed dominate every controller.</strong> Peak forces grow with approach speed for all fixed-gain and learned controllers (98, 173, 241 N for the shared gains at 1, 1.5 and 2 times the speed) and a 1 kg payload adds 10 to 30 N; no controller in the grid is immune.</li>
</ol>""",
    "caveats": """<ul>
<li>The phase-scheduled baseline uses oracle phase labels from the reference; a real system would switch on measured force or estimated contact and would be later and noisier. This makes the baseline stronger than a deployable one on timing and weaker on nothing.</li>
<li>The per-phase gains are the Milestone 1 per-regime optima, which the protocol v1 composite let collapse to near-zero pressing; a per-phase tuning with a press-phase force requirement (protocol v2 proposal) would give a different and probably better scheduled baseline.</li>
<li>Learned controllers are protocol v1 (no disturbance in training) and one seed each; protocol v2 controllers will be substituted when available.</li>
<li>Eight cases per cell; no case diverged.</li>
</ul>""",
    "next": """<p>Re-run the grid with protocol v2 learned controllers and with per-phase gains tuned under a press-phase requirement; add force-triggered switching as the realistic scheduled baseline; and include a 5 ms actuation delay in the plant, which Milestone 3 showed to be decisive for stiff gain sets.</p>""",
}
